          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            The most basic form of the Riemann–Hilbert correspondence states that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flat+connection"} flat
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+bundles"} vector bundles
            on a suitable
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space"} space
            is equivalent to the category of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+systems"} local systems
          %p
            While
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemann-Hilbert+problem"} Hilbert's 21st problem
            has a negative solution, there is a generalized sheaf-theoretical formulation which leads to an equivalence of categories discovered by Mebkhout and a bit later also by
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kashiwara"} Kashiwara
          %h2#statement Statement
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            be a smooth algebraic variety over
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℂ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{C}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} Zar
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝒟
                    %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D(X^\Zar,\mathcal{D}_{X})
            be the derived category of algebraic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D-modules"} D-modules
            on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            (i.e. quasi-coherent
            = succeed "-modules" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝒪
                      %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{O}_{X}
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flat+connection"} flat connection
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi D
                    %mi rh
                    %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} Zar
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝒟
                    %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D_{rh}^{b}(X^\Zar,\mathcal{D}_{X})
            be the full subcategory of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi D
                    %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} Zar
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝒟
                    %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D^{b}(X^\Zar,\mathcal{D}_{X})
            consisting of objects whose cohomology sheaves are regular holonomic algebraic D-modules. Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi X
                    %mi an
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ℂ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D(X^{an},\mathbb{C})
            be the derived category of sheaves of
            = succeed "-vector" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℂ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{C}
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi X
                      %mi an
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X^{an}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi D
                    %mi c
                    %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi X
                    %mi an
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ℂ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D_{c}^{b}(X^{an},\mathbb{C})
            be the full subcategory of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi D
                    %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi X
                    %mi an
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ℂ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D^{b}(X^{an},\mathbb{C})
            consisting of objects whose cohomology sheaves are Zariski constructible.
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℰ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{E}
            be an algebraic D-module and let
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℰ
                      %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} hol
                    %mo =
                    %msubsup
                      %mi 𝒪
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} hol
                    %msub
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi 𝒪
                          %mi X
                    %mi ℰ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{E}^{\hol}=\mathcal{O}_{X}^{\hol}\otimes_{\mathcal{O}_{X}}\mathcal{E}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℰ
                  %mo →
                  %msup
                    %mi Ω
                    %mo *
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi ℰ
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} hol
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{E}\to\Omega^{*}(\mathcal{E}^{\mathrm{hol}})
            determines a functor
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi dR
                  %mo :
                  %mi D
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} Zar
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝒟
                    %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi D
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi X
                    %mi an
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ℂ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} dR: D(X^\Zar,\mathcal{D}_{X})\to D(X^{an},\mathbb{C}).
          %p
          .num_prop
            %h6 Proposition
            %p (Mebkhout, Kashiwara, Beilinson-Bernstein)
            %p
              The restriction of the functor
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi dR
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} dR
              gives an equivalence of categories
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msubsup
                      %mi D
                      %mi rh
                      %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} Zar
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝒟
                      %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mo ∼
                    %msubsup
                      %mi D
                      %mi c
                      %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi X
                      %mi an
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ℂ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D_{rh}^{b}(X^\Zar,\mathcal{D}_{X})\xrightarrow{\sim} D_{c}^{b}(X^{an},\mathbb{C})
            %p
          %h2#use_in_the_geometric_langlands_correspondence Use in the geometric Langlands correspondence
          %p
            The Riemann-Hilbert correspondence allows one to translate the automorphic side of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+Langlands+correspondence"} geometric Langlands correspondence
            from the language of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perverse+sheaves"} perverse sheaves
            (which in turn is motivated by functions on
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Bun
                      %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Bun_{G}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D-modules"} D-modules
            (see
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Frenkel05"} Frenkel05
          %h2#ptorsion_and_padic_riemannhilbert_correspondence p-torsion and p-adic Riemann-Hilbert correspondence
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bhargav+Bhatt"} Bhargav Bhatt
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jacob+Lurie"} Jacob Lurie
            have formulated a version of the Riemann-Hilbert correspondence for finite type schemes over a complete and algebraically closed extension of the
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/p-adic+numbers"} p-adic numbers
          %p
          .num_prop
            %h6 Proposition
            %p
              (
              %a{:href => "#Bhatt21"}> Bhatt21
              , Theorem 5.1)
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              be a complete and algebraically closed extension of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi ℚ
                      %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Q}_{p}
              and let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              be of finite type over
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi 𝒪
                        %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{O}_{C}
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} RH
                    %mo :
                    %msubsup
                      %mi D
                      %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} cons
                      %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mi C
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝔽
                      %mi p
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %msubsup
                      %mi D
                      %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} qc
                      %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %msub
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi 𝒪
                          %mi C
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝒪
                      %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi p
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \RH: D_{\cons}^{b}(X_{C},\mathbb{F}_{p})\to D_{\qc}^{b}(X\otimes_{\mathcal{O}_{C}} \mathcal{O}_{C}/p).
            %p
              This functor commutes with proper pushforward, intertwines
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Verdier+duality"} Verdier duality
              and
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+duality"} Grothendieck duality
              in the almost category, and interacts well with the perverse
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/t-structure"} t-structure
          %p Bhatt and Lurie also have a version with p-adic, instead of p-torsion, coefficients (this time the schemes have to be smooth proper varieties over a finite extension of the p-adic numbers).
          %p
          .num_prop
            %h6 Proposition
            %p
              (
              %a{:href => "#Bhatt21"}> Bhatt21
              , Theorem 5.4)
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi K
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
              be a finite extension of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi ℚ
                      %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Q}_{p}
              and let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              be a smooth proper variety over
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi K
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} RH
                      %mi 𝒟
                    %mo :
                    %msubsup
                      %mi D
                      %mi wHT
                      %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mi C
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi ℚ
                      %mi p
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mi DF
                      %mi coh
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝒟
                      %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \RH_{\mathcal{D}}: D_{wHT}^{b}(X_{C},\mathbb{Q}_{p})\to DF_{coh}(\mathcal{D}_{X})
            %p
              where
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msubsup
                      %mi D
                      %mi wHT
                      %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mi C
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi ℚ
                      %mi p
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D_{wHT}^{b}(X_{C},\mathbb{Q}_{p})
              is a full subcategory of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msubsup
                      %mi D
                      %mi cons
                      %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mi C
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi ℚ
                      %mi p
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D_{cons}^{b}(X_{C},\mathbb{Q}_{p})
              spanned by “weakly Hodge-Tate sheaves” and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi DF
                      %mi coh
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝒟
                      %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} DF_{coh}(\mathcal{D}_{X})
              is a suitable derived category of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D-modules"}
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi 𝒟
                        %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{D}_{X}
                \-modules
              equipped with a “good” filtration. This functor commutes with proper pushforward, intertwines
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Verdier+duality"} Verdier duality
              and
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+duality"} Grothendieck duality
              in the almost category, and interacts well with the perverse
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/t-structure"} t-structure
          %p
            The construction of Bhatt and Lurie is as follows. Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi K
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
            be a discretely-valued p-adic field and let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            be a smooth projective variety over
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi K
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
            with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒳
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{X}
            its associated rigid analytic space. Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℱ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{F}
            be a constructible sheaf over
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℱ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{F}
            is
            %em weakly de Rham
            if the stalk
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℱ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{F}
            is a weakly de Rham Galois representation. More generally if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℱ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{F}
            is an object of the derived category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi D
                    %mi c
                    %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi ℚ
                    %mi p
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D_{c}^{b}(X,\mathbb{Q}_{p})
            we say that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℱ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{F}
            is
            %em weakly de Rham
            if its cohomology sheaves are weakly de Rham.
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ν
                  %mo :
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝒳
                    %mi proet
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝒳
                    %mi et
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \nu:\mathcal{X}_{proet}\to \mathcal{X}_{et}
            be the projection and let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi ℬ
                    %mi dR
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{B}_{dR}
            be the de Rham period sheaf on
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝒳
                      %mi proet
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{X}_{proet}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℱ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{F}
            is weakly de Rham, we define
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi RH
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ℱ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo :
                  %mo =
                  %mi R
                  %msub
                    %mi ν
                    %mo *
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi ν
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                  %mi ℱ
                  %msub
                    %mover
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mo ^
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi ℚ
                        %mi p
                  %msub
                    %mi ℬ
                    %mi dR
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %mi u
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} RH(\mathcal{F}):=R\nu_{*}(\nu^{-1}\mathcal{F}\widehat{\otimes}_{\mathbb{Q}_{p}}\mathcal{B}_{dR}\langle u\rangle)
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lucas+Mann"} Lucas Mann
            has also formulated a version of the p-torsion Riemann-Hilbert correspondence for small v-stacks as part of his proof of mod p Poincare duality for
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rigid+analytic+spaces"} rigid analytic spaces
            = succeed "-modules," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi φ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \varphi
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/solid+modules"} solid modules
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/almost+modules"} almost modules
            over
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi 𝒪
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} +
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi π
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{O}_{X}^{+}/\pi
            together with an action of the Frobenius
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi φ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \varphi
            = succeed "-sheaves" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝔽
                      %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{F}_{p}
            = succeed "-categories" do
